 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
 A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
