 \paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.
 
-\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget sco